TIP(1)              UNIX Programmer's Manual                 TIP(1)



NAME
     tip, cu - serial terminal

SYNOPSIS
     tip [ tty00 | tty01 | /dev/ttyNN ]
     cu [ tty00 | tty01 | /dev/ttyNN ]

DESCRIPTION
     _T_i_p connects the console to a serial line and passes
     characters both ways: keys you type go out the line (no
     local echo; the remote side echoes), and bytes arriving on
     the line are shown on the console.  _C_u is the same program
     under its other name.

     The line defaults to /dev/tty01, the modem port.  A single
     argument names another line: a bare name (tty00) is looked
     up under /dev, an absolute path is used as given.  Line
     speed and framing come from the IIgs Control Panel; FGX
     programs the port from those settings when the serial
     module loads.

     A tilde (~) typed at the beginning of a line is an escape:

     ~.        drop the connection and exit (also ~^D)

     ~~        send one literal tilde

     ~>        send a local file raw out the line.  Prompts for
               the file name.

     ~<        toggle capture: incoming bytes are written to a
               local file (recorded raw, before CR/LF
               normalization).  Prompts for the file name; a
               second ~< closes it.

     ~?        print a summary of the escapes

     Any other character after the tilde sends the tilde and
     that character down the line.

     Incoming CR/LF pairs are collapsed to a single newline so
     remote lines do not double-space on the console.

DIFFERENCES FROM BSD
     The historical tip's remote(5)/phones(5) system database,
     ACU dialers, uucp lock files, variables (~s), shell and
     file-transfer escapes bound to a remote UNIX (~!, ~c, ~t,
     ~p, ~|, ~$), BREAK (~#), and job control (~^Y, ~^Z) are not
     implemented: this tip is one process driving two fixed
     lines, with no modems to dial.

FGX                          FGX-native                          1
